\section{Full Ablation Results}
\label{sec:ablation_full}

Table~\ref{tab:ablation_full} reports the complete RSFT ablation across scoring functions, top-$k$ values, and random seeds. RSFT CED $k{=}1$ achieves a mean Ign-F1 of 44.66 with low variance across four seeds (standard deviation 0.49), confirming the robustness of the improvement over the SFT baseline (39.74). Increasing $k$ to 3 slightly degrades performance (43.64 Ign-F1), consistent with the intuition that stricter selection yields higher-quality training data. The flat-NLI scoring variant produces comparable Rel-F1 (mean 0.4561 vs.\ 0.4563 for CED, a difference of 0.02pp). Both CED and flat-NLI improve substantially over the SFT baseline while maintaining or improving Evi-F1, confirming that the RSFT gains are driven by quality-based data selection rather than by the specific contrastive formulation.

\paragraph{NLI scoring comparison.} Across all available seeds, CED RSFT achieves mean Rel-F1 of 0.4563 (seeds 42, 43, 44, 45: range 0.4514--0.4621), and flat-NLI RSFT achieves mean Rel-F1 of 0.4561 (seeds 42, 43: range 0.4535--0.4586). The 0.02pp difference suggests comparable performance: CED's contrastive design does not substantially outperform simpler NLI scoring for RSFT data selection. We adopt CED as the default because its contrastive formulation produces per-triple discrimination scores that support interpretable error analysis.

\paragraph{Top-$k$ selection.} Best-of-8 ($k{=}1$) yields mean Ign-F1 44.66 and Rel-F1 0.4563, while top-3-of-8 ($k{=}3$) produces 43.64 and 0.4454. The selected training data confirms the quality gap: $k{=}1$ data has mean extraction F1 of 0.6157 and mean CED of 0.6683, versus 0.5761 and 0.6205 for $k{=}3$. Despite $k{=}3$ providing 2.96$\times$ more training examples, the additional lower-ranked samples introduce noise that degrades final performance.

